# 3. Extraction pipeline

## Stage 1 — Preflight classification

Determine:

```text
media_type
page_count
image_dimensions
orientation
language candidates
currency candidates
quality score
```

Detect whether a file is:

- a menu;
- an advertisement;
- a receipt;
- a business card;
- an unrelated image;
- a duplicate upload;
- a menu page with missing continuation pages.

If the upload is not a menu, respond clearly:

> I couldn’t identify a menu in this file. Please send a clear menu photo or PDF.

## Stage 2 — PDF text and layout extraction

For PDFs:

1. extract embedded text when present;
2. render pages to images when layout or text quality requires it;
3. detect columns, sections, price alignment, and item grouping;
4. preserve page and bounding-box references;
5. send only the relevant page/image chunks to the model.

Never rely on plain PDF text alone when prices may be visually aligned with the wrong dish.

## Stage 3 — OCR

Use OCR for:

- Arabic script;
- English;
- French;
- Arabizi where recognizable;
- Arabic-Indic numerals;
- price strings;
- option groups;
- footnotes.

Store raw OCR separately from normalized extraction.

```text
raw_ocr_text
normalized_text
language
page_number
bounding_box
ocr_confidence
```

## Stage 4 — Vision-language extraction

The model receives:

```text
merchant_id
vendor_id
page number
image or rendered page
OCR text
known business language
known currency settings
existing menu candidates
```

It returns structured candidates only. No direct database writes.

## Stage 5 — Normalization

Normalize:

- Arabic and English names;
- spelling and transliteration variants;
- sections;
- prices;
- size/quantity variants;
- required and optional options;
- ingredients listed by the merchant;
- availability phrases;
- “market price” or missing price flags.

Never invent a missing price. Mark it `needs_merchant_input`.

## Stage 6 — Duplicate detection

Compare extracted candidates against:

- current vendor menu;
- other pages in the same upload;
- previous extraction versions;
- normalized item name;
- section;
- price;
- option group;
- image similarity where available.

Do not automatically merge two items with similar names when their sizes or prices differ.

## Stage 7 — Merchant review

Send a compact WhatsApp summary first:

> I found **42 menu items** in 5 pages. I detected prices mainly in **USD**. 6 items need your confirmation and 4 look like possible duplicates. I’ll send the review in sections.

Then show review batches of 10–15 items with actions:

```text
Approve item
Edit item
Skip item
Approve all in section
```

For a large menu, direct the merchant to the portal for table editing while keeping WhatsApp approval available for smaller corrections.

## Stage 8 — Publish as draft, then live

Use two separate states:

```text
extracted_draft
merchant_reviewed
published_hidden
published_live
```

An extraction may be saved as a draft without becoming visible to customers. Publishing live requires merchant confirmation and the menu go-live gate.

