# 4. Structured extraction schema

Use a strict JSON schema similar to:

```json
{
  "menu_document": {
    "language_candidates": ["ar-LB", "en"],
    "currency_candidates": ["USD", "LBP"],
    "pages_processed": 5,
    "quality_issues": [],
    "questions_for_merchant": []
  },
  "items": [
    {
      "source_ref": "page2:block7",
      "name_original": "شاورما دجاج",
      "name_en": "Chicken Shawarma",
      "name_ar": "شاورما دجاج",
      "section": "Shawarma",
      "description_original": "...",
      "description_en": null,
      "price_value": 6,
      "price_currency": "USD",
      "price_text_original": "$6",
      "price_confidence": 0.99,
      "name_confidence": 0.96,
      "ingredients_listed": ["chicken", "garlic sauce"],
      "allergens_mentioned": [],
      "option_groups": [],
      "availability_text": null,
      "needs_merchant_review": false,
      "warnings": []
    }
  ]
}
```

Required fields must be explicit. Use `null` plus a warning instead of guessing.

## Confidence policy

| Confidence | Behavior |
|---|---|
| 0.95–1.00 | Highlight as high-confidence; merchant still approves publication |
| 0.80–0.94 | Include in review with warning |
| 0.60–0.79 | Require merchant edit or explicit approval |
| Below 0.60 | Do not suggest publication; request a clearer image/manual input |

Confidence is not truth. A visually clear but semantically ambiguous price can still be wrong.

