# 9. Order tracking architecture

## 9.1 Tracking is event-driven

The current tracking page already provides a public tracking route, status timeline, map, and periodic refresh. The production strategy should evolve from a primarily polling/haversine model to:

```text
order state event
+ driver location event
+ route estimate
+ notification outbox
+ customer tracking view
```

Use Supabase Realtime for active tracking where feasible, with polling fallback for weak networks or suspended sockets.

## 9.2 Customer tracking data boundary

A public tracking token should expose only:

```text
order short code
restaurant name
sanitized status
estimated time range
last known driver position or coarse position
route progress
delivery village/landmark summary
support action
```

Do not expose:

- driver phone number;
- exact driver identity beyond approved display name;
- other customers in a batch;
- full customer address to public viewers;
- internal notes;
- cash ledger;
- private event metadata.

Use a short-lived signed tracking token instead of relying only on a guessable order UUID.

## 9.3 Tracking states

Customer-facing states should be simpler than internal states:

```text
received
accepted
being_prepared
ready
driver_assigned
on_the_way
nearby
delivered
issue
cancelled
```

Internal states can remain more detailed:

```text
pending
accepted
preparing
ready
assigned
at_pickup
picked_up
en_route
at_dropoff
awaiting_customer
delivered
not_collected
exception
cancelled
rejected
```

## 9.4 Timeline events

Each event includes:

```text
event_id
order_id
previous_status
new_status
actor_type
actor_id
created_at
source
metadata
```

The customer view should render the timeline from trusted events, not derive it from the current order row alone.

