# 15. Lebanon zone rollout model

## Phase 1 — Shouf operational core

Start with a small connected cluster rather than scattered coverage:

```text
Baakline
Deir el Qamar
Beiteddine
Kfarhim
Moukhtara
```

Activate corridors only after route testing, not merely because coordinates are nearby.

## Phase 2 — adjacent connected routes

Expand to neighboring villages when:

- at least one merchant can serve the route;
- route time is measured;
- a driver can cover the route without excessive deadhead;
- the delivery fee reflects actual operating cost.

## Phase 3 — regional hubs

For Metn/Keserwan, Beirut, North/Akkar, South, and Bekaa, create separate operational hubs rather than treating Lebanon as one dispatch pool.

Each hub needs:

```text
local merchant manager
local dispatch coverage
route matrix
driver pool
cash reconciliation point
support escalation
```

Do not advertise all 2,087 imported vendors as one operational marketplace before hub coverage exists.

---

# 16. Dynamic zone capacity

A zone can be active but temporarily limited.

Track:

```text
active drivers
active orders
unassigned orders
average acceptance time
restaurant capacity
late-risk score
weather/road status
```

Actions:

```text
normal
limited menu
longer ETA
scheduled orders only
pause new orders
```

If the last active driver goes offline while orders are active:

- do not cancel automatically;
- stop accepting new orders if no replacement exists;
- show active customers honest ETAs;
- alert dispatch;
- recruit or reassign a driver;
- use a human escalation for affected orders.

